Practical-6
Implementation of chain matrix multiplication using dynamic programming.
Definition
Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM) is a Dynamic Programming technique used to determine the most efficient order of multiplying a sequence of matrices.
Since matrix multiplication is associative, different parenthesizations produce the same result but may require different numbers of scalar multiplications.
MCM finds the order that minimizes the total number of scalar multiplications.
Time Complexity
O(n³)
There are three nested loops:
•	One for the chain length 
•	One for the starting matrix i 
•	One for every possible split position k 
Therefore, the overall time complexity is O(n³).
Space Complexity
O(n²)
The algorithm uses a 2D DP table dp[i][j] to store the minimum multiplication cost for every possible matrix chain.
Therefore, the space complexity is O(n²).
Conclusion
Thus, Matrix Chain Multiplication using Dynamic Programming efficiently finds the optimal order of multiplying
a sequence of matrices while minimizing the number of scalar multiplications.
The DP approach avoids repeated calculations by storing solutions to smaller subproblems and has a time complexity of O(n³) and a space complexity of O(n²).

